<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barbary Coast</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Barbary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Barbary Coast was the name used by Europeans from the sixteenth to the nineteenth century for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>. The term derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>. Its ports were home to the Barbary corsairs, who raided shipping and coasts and took captives for ransom or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> until the French conquest of Algiers in 1830 ended the practice.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barbary-coast.jpg" alt="Barbary Coast" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dirk Jansz van Santen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B13_057-BARBARIA.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
